NAME
       podman-run - create and start a new container

SYNOPSIS
       podman run [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND [ARGUMENT...]]

DESCRIPTION
       podman run makes a new container from IMAGE and starts it. When the
       image is not stored locally, it is pulled first. Without COMMAND the
       container runs the image's default command (CMD / ENTRYPOINT).

       In the foreground, the output appears in your terminal and podman run
       returns when the command ends. With -d (detached) it prints the new
       container's ID and the container keeps running in the background.

       A container's own files disappear with the container. Data that must
       last goes into a volume: a host directory or a named volume mounted
       into the container with -v.

OPTIONS
       -d, --detach
              Run in the background and print the container ID.

       --name NAME
              Name the container (otherwise podman makes up a name). Names
              are easier to use in later commands and in systemd units.

       -p, --publish HOSTPORT:CONTAINERPORT
              Make a port of the container reachable on the host, such as
              -p 8080:80. Rootless users can only use host ports from 1024 up.
              Open the host port in the firewall when other machines must
              reach it.

       -v, --volume HOSTDIR:CONTAINERDIR[:OPTIONS]
              Mount a host directory (or a named volume) inside the
              container. Options, separated by commas: ro (read-only), Z
              (relabel the directory for this container only, with the
              SELinux type container_file_t), z (relabel for sharing between
              containers). Without Z or z, SELinux usually blocks the
              container from reading the directory.

       -e, --env NAME=VALUE
              Set an environment variable in the container (repeatable).
              Database images such as rhel9/mariadb-105 read their settings
              this way.

       -i, -t, -it
              Keep standard input open and give the container a terminal,
              for an interactive shell.

       --rm   Remove the container when it ends.

       -u, --user USER[:GROUP]
              Run the command as this user inside the container.

       -w, --workdir DIR
              The working directory inside the container.

       --restart POLICY
              What podman does when the container exits: no, on-failure,
              always. For starting at boot, use a systemd unit instead.

       --replace
              If a container with the same name exists, remove it first.

       --network, --hostname, --label, --memory, --entrypoint, --privileged
              Network mode, host name, labels, memory limit, a different
              entry point, extra privileges.

EXAMPLES
       Run a command once and throw the container away:

           podman run --rm registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/ubi cat /etc/redhat-release

       An interactive shell in a new container:

           podman run -it --name test registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/ubi /bin/bash

       A web server whose pages live in the user's ~/web directory:

           mkdir ~/web
           echo hello > ~/web/index.html
           podman run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 -v ~/web:/var/www/html:Z \
               registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24

       A database with settings in environment variables and its data in a
       host directory (MariaDB runs as UID 27 inside, so the directory is
       given to that UID first):

           mkdir ~/dbdata
           podman unshare chown 27:27 ~/dbdata
           podman run -d --name db -p 3306:3306 -v ~/dbdata:/var/lib/mysql:Z \
               -e MYSQL_USER=student -e MYSQL_PASSWORD=student \
               -e MYSQL_DATABASE=inventory -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=redhat \
               registry.lab.example.com/rhel9/mariadb-105

NOTES
       A rootless container's processes run under UIDs from the user's
       range in /etc/subuid. When the image's program runs as a non-root
       user (httpd-24 uses UID 1001), a mounted directory may need to be
       owned by that UID inside the container: podman unshare chown -R
       1001:1001 ~/web.

